---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AdSlot from "../components/AdSlot.astro";
import { GITHUB, CONTACT, INSTAGRAM } from "../consts";
---

<Base
  title="The studio"
  description="What DynShift is, how it decides a tool is finished, what it publishes under which licence, who runs it and how to reach it."
>
  <section class="wrap prose">
    <h1>The studio</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      DynShift is an independent software studio. It makes small, focused tools,
      publishes most of them as open source, and does not call one finished
      until it has been measured doing the job it was built for.
    </p>

    <h2>What it makes</h2>
    <p>
      Two kinds of thing so far. <strong>Products</strong> that a person installs
      and uses, of which <a href="/projects/phonepad/">PhonePad</a> is the first
      to reach a public release. And <strong>developer libraries</strong>:
      <a href="/projects/layr/">Layr</a> for React, and a set of
      <a href="/packages/">Flutter packages</a> on pub.dev, each solving one
      problem that came up while building apps.
    </p>
    <p>
      Software is the starting point, not the boundary. The intention is to grow
      from tools into games, and further out into hardware. Each new project
      appears on this site when it ships, not when it is announced.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      One thing at a time. A project is worked on until it is done, then it is
      released, then the next one starts. There is no portfolio of half-built
      ideas competing for attention, because a tool that is nearly finished is
      not useful to anyone.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Finished has a definition.</strong> A tool leaves the studio when
      three things are true:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It does the whole job it set out to do. PhonePad, for example, shipped
        with game rumble reaching the phone, because a controller that cannot
        rumble is not the controller it claimed to be.
      </li>
      <li>
        It has been measured, not assumed to work. The latency, packet rate and
        loss figures on PhonePad's site each come from a recorded run on real
        hardware, with the method written down next to the result. Where
        something was not measured, the documentation says so.
      </li>
      <li>
        It reads well enough that somebody else could maintain it. Comments
        explain why, not what; the failures that shaped a design are recorded
        in the code that fixed them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That last point is why so much of the work is public. Source that will be
      read by strangers is written more carefully than source that will not.
    </p>

    <AdSlot />

    <h2>Open source, and which licence</h2>
    <p>
      Products are published under the <strong>GNU GPL-3.0</strong>. Anyone can
      read, build, modify and redistribute them, and any derivative must stay
      open on the same terms. PhonePad is GPL-3.0 and, as its own site says, the
      controller is not going behind a paywall.
    </p>
    <p>
      Libraries are published under the <strong>MIT licence</strong>, because a
      library is only useful if it can be used inside anything, including closed
      software. Layr and all four Flutter packages are MIT.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public code lives under <a href={GITHUB} rel="noopener">MNBLabs</a> on
      GitHub, which is the studio's organisation account. Issues, discussions
      and pull requests are open on every public repository, and contributions
      arrive there.
    </p>

    <h2>Names</h2>
    <p>
      DynShift and PhonePad are unregistered trademarks of DynShift. Neither is
      registered anywhere, and the sites do not pretend otherwise. Product names
      belonging to other companies (Xbox, Windows, Flutter, React) are their
      owners' marks and are used only to say what a tool works with.
    </p>

    <h2>Who</h2>
    <p>
      DynShift is run by its founder,
      <a href="https://github.com/nishanbhuinya" rel="noopener">Nishan Bhuinya</a>,
      who designs and builds the products, writes the documentation and answers
      the issues. The studio is on Instagram as
      <a href={INSTAGRAM} rel="noopener">@dynshift</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Money</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing DynShift has released so far costs anything, and the free
      versions are the products, not demos of them. Anyone who found a tool
      useful can help pay for the time behind it, through
      <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/MNBLabs" rel="noopener">GitHub Sponsors</a>
      or <a href="https://ko-fi.com/nishanbhuinya" rel="noopener">Ko-fi</a>.
      If paid extras ever appear they will be additions, never a tax on what
      already works.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site is also funded by advertising, which is where the rest of the
      time comes from. It runs no analytics and collects nothing itself; what
      Google's advertising does collect, and what choices you have about it, is
      in the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>. Each product site has its
      own.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      For anything about a specific project, its GitHub repository is the
      fastest route: bugs as issues, questions and ideas as discussions. For
      everything else, <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.6em;
  }
</style>
